<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Mansfeld</h1><p class="st-meta">1069 CE – 1780 CE (approx.) · capital Mansfeld</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A county on the eastern edge of the Harz whose copper mines made it rich and in whose town Martin Luther was born.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Counts of Mansfeld appear around 1069, with their castle above the town of Mansfeld east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>. The original line died out in 1229, and the lands passed to the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a>, who took the name Mansfeld and ruled for the rest of the county's history.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mansfeld's wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> shale mining, one of the largest copper industries in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> was born in nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a> in 1483 and grew up in Mansfeld, where his father ran smelters. The counts adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> early, and Luther died in Eisleben in 1546 after mediating a dispute between them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Heavy debts and repeated divisions among many branches weakened the counts, and from 1570 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a> took the county into sequestration. The last count died in 1780, and the lands were divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-mansfeld.jpg" alt="County of Mansfeld" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: Wappen gemeinsam erstellt von LeoDavid und DORADO · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mansfeld-St-Wappen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1069 CE – 1780 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mansfeld</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albert VII of Mansfeld</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle and Early New High German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mansfeld</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1424&amp;focus=county-of-mansfeld" aria-label="County of Mansfeld on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4939 392 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Mansfeld lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5259 648L5229 694M5259 648L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 648h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 648h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mansfeld. <a href="/atlas?year=1424&amp;focus=county-of-mansfeld">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

